$t_{d}=(w_{f}/{\rm step})t_{exp}=4.0\times10^{-4}\,{\rm s}$ and the
corresponding single-energy surface kerma per step is

\begin{equation*}
K_{t}\approx\dot K_{a}\,(w_{f}/{\rm step})t_{exp}
        \approx1.9\times10^{-4}\times4.0\times10^{-4}
        \approx7\!-\!8\times10^{-8}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.08\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per step, i.e. $\approx 0.2\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per step per three energies,
the conventional “average over the scanned field” figure.  The
$3$–$4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ value above is the peak surface kerma experienced
by a point while it is actually inside the $0.30\,{\rm mm}$ focal spot
for the quoted $20\,{\rm ms}$ dwell. ∎

public_text.3:
In an air walled chamber the charge which is collected in a time dt is
the charge which is produced by the energy which is put into the mass
$m_{a}$ of air which is seen by the beam

\begin{equation*}
dE=K_{\rm air}\;m_{a}\;dt\qquad 
dQ=\frac{dE}{W} \;\;\Longrightarrow\;\; I=\frac{K_{\rm air}\;m_{a}\;e}
{W}
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the conventional value $W/e\simeq 33.97\;{\rm V}$ for air

\begin{equation*}
K_{\rm air}\equiv\dot D_{{\rm air}}=\frac{I\,W}{m_{a}} ,
\end{equation*}
the $W$ being used in the conventional $W=33.97\;{\rm J\,C^{-1}}$ units.
For the beam which is seen by the chamber

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\times10^{-3}{\rm m}\times 6.0\times10^{-3}{\rm
m}=1.8\times10^{-6}\;{\rm m^{2}}
\end{equation*}
and the mass of air which is in the beam in the  $L=15.1\;{\rm cm}$ long
chamber is

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}= \rho_{a}A_{f}L
     =1.293\times10^{-3}\,{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times
       0.03\,{\rm cm}\times0.60\,{\rm cm}\times15.1\,{\rm cm}
     \simeq3.5\times10^{-4}\;{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}\;{\rm kg}\; .
\end{equation*}
Hence the air kerma rate in the focus is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{{\rm air}}=\dot D_{{\rm air}}
 =\frac{I\,W}{m_{a}}
 =\frac{2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97{\rm J\,C^{-1}}}
        {3.51\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}}
 \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
in the $33\;{\rm keV}$ beam.  With $ (\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\approx
(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ the air kerma is an essentially good estimate of
the absorbed dose rate in soft tissue in the focus.

If $t_{d}$ is the time a given skin element spends in the $w_{f}$ wide
focused beam the dose received by the element in one dwell is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{{\rm air}}\;t_{d}\; .
\end{equation*}
For a continuous horizontal scan

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v}=w_{f}\frac{t_{e}}{\Delta s}
      =\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta s/t_{e}}
\end{equation*}
is the beam width divided by the scan speed.  The quantity

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}\equiv\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta s/t_{e}}
\end{equation*}
is quoted in the statement as $0.02\;{\rm s}$.  Hence the dose
imparted to a skin element during the time it traverses the $0.30\;{\rm
mm}$ focus is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}\simeq\dot D_{{\rm air}}\,t_{d}
       \approx 1.93\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}\;{\rm s}
       \approx 3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\;{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}
i.e. $\sim 4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per mono-energetic dwell, $\approx 0.4\;{\rm
mrad}$, in the $33\;{\rm keV}$ beam.  This is the usual “per projection’’
surface kerma which is quoted for the synchrotron micro-beam.  The
energy-absorption coefficient of soft tissue is taken to be that of air
so that the air kerma is a direct estimate of the tissue absorbed dose
at the entrance surface.

If the dose is summed over the whole field which is built up by
scanning the $w_{m}=50\;{\rm mm}$ monochromator acceptance with the
$w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ focus the number of independent dwell positions
in the horizontal direction is

\begin{equation*}
N_{h}\simeq\frac{w_{m}}{w_{f}}=\frac{50}{0.30}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\; .
\end{equation*}
The integrated, i.e. field averaged, skin dose which is delivered in
building up the $50\;{\rm mm}\times 6\;{\rm mm}$ radiograph is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{{\rm field}}\simeq N_{h}D_{1}
      \approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}{\rm Gy}
      \approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}{\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\;{\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}
per mono-energetic exposure in the $33\;{\rm keV}$ beam, essentially
the dose normally quoted for a full synchrotron multiple-energy scan.
The $\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per dwell is the dose actually experienced
by any single skin element as it passes once through the $0.30\;{\rm
mm}$ focus; the $ \sim0.6\;{\rm mGy}$ figure is the conventionally quoted
field-integrated entrance surface dose for the $50\;{\rm mm}$ field
built up by scanning.  With the usual three or four energy points used
in multiple-energy imaging the total entrance skin dose is a few mGy,
i.e. well below conventional diagnostic levels.  The $50\;{\rm mm}$ and
$6\;{\rm mm}$ monochromator dimensions and the $0.328\;{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$
mass energy absorption coefficient are the usual geometrical normalisation
and are not required for the per-dwell estimate once the chamber
calibration $I\,W/m_{a}$ is used.

public_text.1:
In an air ionisation chamber

$$K_{\rm air}= \frac{{\rm d}E/{\rm d}t}{m}= \frac{I\,W/e}{m}$$

is the air kerma rate in the beam.  With $W/e\simeq 33.97\;{\rm V}$

\begin{equation*}
\frac{{\rm d}E}{{\rm d}t}=I\,\frac{W}{e}=2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97
      \simeq6.8\times10^{-11}\;{\rm J\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

is the power absorbed in the air which is in the beam.  At the
focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\;{\rm mm^{2}}=1.8\times10^{-2}\;{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

so that with the $15.1\;{\rm cm}$ active length

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L=0.018\times15.1\simeq0.272\;{\rm cm^{3}},\qquad 
m_{a}= \rho_{a}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times0.272\;{\rm cm^{3}}
      \simeq3.5\times10^{-4}\;{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}\;{\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

and

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \frac{I\,W/e}{m_{a}}\simeq
\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}\simeq1.9\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in air at the focus.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{\rm tissue}\simeq
(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}$ this is the tissue kerma rate in the $0.30\times
6.0\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ spot.  In a raster the dwell time of a material point
in the spot is

\begin{equation*}
\tau_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/t_{e}}
\end{equation*}

i.e. the time quoted in the statement, $\tau_{d}\simeq0.02\;{\rm s}$.
The dose actually received by a point in one pass through the beam is
therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot K_{a}\,\tau_{d}\simeq1.9\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times
2.0\times10^{-2}\;{\rm s}\simeq3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\;{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per energy.  This is $\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per monochromatic exposure
at the focus, $\dot K_{a}\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ peak.  
In terms of the photon fluence this is

\begin{equation*}
\Phi =\frac{\dot K_{a}}{E(\mu_{en}/\rho)}\approx
\frac{1.9\times10^{-4}}{5.29\times10^{-15}\times0.0328}
\approx1.1\times10^{12}\;{\rm m^{-2}s^{-1}}\approx1.1\times10^{8}\;
{\rm cm^{-2}s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

at 33 keV, and

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \Phi\,E(\mu_{en}/\rho)\,\tau_{d}
\end{equation*}

is the usual air$\simeq$tissue kerma expression.

For a conventional three–energy material decomposition the point dose is
$\simeq 3D_{1}\approx1.2\times10^{-5}\,$Gy, i.e. $\sim10\;\mu{\rm Gy}$,
and the mean surface dose delivered to the rastered field whose mean
illuminated area is the $50\times6\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ monochromator footprint
is the same $D_{1}$ per energy, $ \approx 4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy,
about $8\!-\!12\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ for the usual $2\!-\!3$ energy set, which
is the conventionally quoted synchrotron scanning surface dose.  The
$A_{m}/A_{f}=50/0.30\simeq167$ focusing factor cancels in the mean
surface dose quoted for the whole raster so that the often quoted
“cumulative’’ raster dose is essentially the $ \dot K_{a}\tau_{d}$
value above.  This is $\sim 4\times10^{-6}\,$Gy per monochromatic pass
at the $2.0\;{\rm pA}$ operating point, some $0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$
peak kerma rate at the 33 keV focus.

3.text:
In an air walled chamber

* $W/e\simeq33.97\;{\rm V}$ for air

$\dot E=I\,W/e$ is the power put into the air which is seen by the beam

\begin{equation*}
\dot E=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97{\rm V}
      \simeq6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm J\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in the beam. At the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}
     =1.8\times10^{-2}\ {\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

so that with the $L=15.1\,{\rm cm}$ active length

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L\simeq0.272\ {\rm cm^{3}},\qquad 
m_{a}= \rho_{a}V_{f}
     =1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times0.272{\rm cm^{3}}
     \simeq3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

is the mass of air which is irradiated.  The air kerma rate on the
axis of the $0.30\times6.0\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ focus is therefore

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \frac{I\,W/e}{m_{a}}
        \simeq\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
        \simeq1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
        \approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in air.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}=0.328\,
{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$ at 33 keV

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}\simeq\dot D_{t}\simeq\Phi E(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}
\end{equation*}

is the collision kerma / absorbed dose rate in soft tissue in the
focus, $\Phi\approx1.1\times10^{8}\ {\rm cm^{-2}s^{-1}}$ in the
$0.3\times6\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ spot.

For a point of tissue which is translated through the stationary
focus the illumination time is

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/t_{e}}
\end{equation*}

which is quoted as

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=w_{f}/\bigl(\Delta x/t_{e}\bigr)=0.020\ {\rm s}
\end{equation*}

for the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ spot.  The kerma actually received by
that surface element in one monochromatic pass is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot K_{a}\,t_{d}
     \simeq1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}
     \simeq3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\approx3\!-\!4\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx0.4\;{\rm mrad}$ per $33\,{\rm keV}$
projection at the $2.0\;{\rm pA}$ operating point, essentially the
air$\simeq$tissue entrance surface dose.  This is the peak dose a
given skin element experiences while it is actually inside the
$0.30\,{\rm mm}$ focal spot for the quoted $20\,{\rm ms}$ dwell.

In particular

* $\dot K_{a}\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ on axis,
* $D_{1}\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per monochromatic dwell.

For the conventional 2–3 energy multiple-energy acquisition the point
dose simply adds, $D_{N}\simeq N D_{1}$, i.e. $\simeq7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$
for a two-energy set and $\simeq1.0\!-\!1.2\times10^{-5}\ {\rm Gy}\approx
10\!-\!12\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ for the usual three-energy material
decomposition, the $\mu{\rm Gy}$ per projection level normally quoted
for synchrotron micro-imaging.

Remark on the field-integrated number.  The $50\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm
mm}$ monochromator fan is imaged to the $0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm
mm}$ focus, $N_{h}=W_{m}/w_{f}=50/0.30\simeq1.67\times10^{2}$ independent
dwell positions.  The total time needed to raster the whole fan is
$T_{\rm scan}=N_{h}t_{d}\simeq3.3\,{\rm s}$ and the energy deposited in
the moving focal spot while the $50\,{\rm mm}$ field is built up is

\begin{equation*}
D_{\rm field}\simeq N_{h}D_{1}\simeq\dot K_{a}T_{\rm scan}
        \approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
        \approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\ {\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

per monochromatic fan scan, $\approx0.64\;{\rm mGy}$ with the numbers
above.  This $\sim0.6\;{\rm mGy}$ is the conventionally quoted
scan-integrated entrance surface dose for the $50\,{\rm mm}$ field; the
$ \approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy, $\sim10\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ for a
2–3-energy set, is the physically relevant dose actually experienced
by a single tissue element as it passes once through the $0.30\,{\rm mm}$
focus. ∎

1.text:
In the air walled chamber the charge which is collected is the charge
produced by the energy put into the air which is in the beam

\begin{equation*}
\dot E =I\,\frac{W}{e}, \qquad \frac{W}{e}\simeq 33.97\;{\rm V},
\end{equation*}

so that

\begin{equation*}
\dot E =2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97\,{\rm V}
      \simeq6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm J\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

is the power absorbed in the air intersected by the beam.  In the
focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}\ {\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

and with the $L=15.1\;{\rm cm}$ active length

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L\simeq0.272\ {\rm cm^{3}},\qquad
m_{a}= \rho_{a}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}\times0.272
     \simeq3.5\times10^{-4}\ {\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

so that the air kerma rate on the axis of the $0.30\times6.0\;{\rm
mm^{2}}$ focal spot is the usual $I\,W/e$ air kerma

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \frac{I\,W/e}{m_{a}}
        \simeq\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
        \simeq1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\approx0.19\ {\rm
mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in air.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}=0.328\,
{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$ at $33\;{\rm keV}$ this is the collision kerma, and
essentially the absorbed dose rate in soft tissue in electronic
equilibrium in the focal spot.

The dwell of a material point in the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ wide spot
in a continuous horizontal raster is

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/t_{e}},
\end{equation*}

the quantity quoted in the statement, $t_{d}=0.020\;{\rm s}$.  The
kerma, and with $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ the
absorbed dose, actually received by a surface element during one pass
through the beam and for one monochromatic energy is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot K_{a}\,t_{d}
     \simeq1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}
     \simeq3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per $33\;{\rm keV}$ exposure.  In fluence terms

\begin{equation*}
\Phi =\frac{\dot K_{a}}{E(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}}
     \approx\frac{1.9\times10^{-4}}
                {5.29\times10^{-15}\times0.0328}
     \approx1.1\times10^{8}\ {\rm cm^{-2}s^{-1}},
\end{equation*}

$D_{1}=\Phi E(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}t_{d}$, i.e. $\approx0.39\;{\rm mrad}$
per projection.  For a conventional $N_{E}$-energy material
decomposition the point dose simply adds,

\begin{equation*}
D_{N}\simeq N_{E}D_{1},
\end{equation*}

e.g. $D_{2}\simeq7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ for two energies and
$D_{3}\simeq1.1\!-\!1.2\times10^{-5}\ {\rm Gy}\approx10\!-\!12\;\mu{\rm
Gy}$ for the usual three energy set, the $\sim\mu{\rm Gy}$ per
projection dose normally quoted for synchrotron micro-imaging.

The $50\;{\rm mm}\times6\;{\rm mm}$ monochromator acceptance is
geometrically compressed by $M=w_{f}/W_{m}=0.30/50.0=6.0\times10^{-3}$ to
the $0.30\times6.0\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ focus.  The number of independent
focal positions required to build up the $W_{m}=50\;{\rm mm}$ field is

\begin{equation*}
N_{h}\simeq\frac{W_{m}}{w_{f}}=\frac{50}{0.30}\approx1.67\times10^{2},
\end{equation*}

so that the time needed to scan the whole fan is
$T_{scan}=N_{h}t_{d}\approx3.33\;{\rm s}$.  The energy which is
delivered to the moving focal spot while the $50\;{\rm mm}$ fan is
built up is

\begin{equation*}
D_{field}\simeq \dot K_{a}T_{scan}=N_{h}D_{1}
          \approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
          \approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\ {\rm
mGy}
\end{equation*}

per monochromatic energy, $\approx0.64\;{\rm mGy}$ with the numbers
above, i.e. the conventionally quoted field-integrated entrance surface
dose for the $50\;{\rm mm}\times6\;{\rm mm}$ Laue fan.  The $D_{1}
\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per $20\;{\rm ms}$ dwell is the dose actually
experienced by any single skin element as it traverses the
$0.30\times6.0\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ microbeam; the $ \sim0.6\;{\rm mGy}$ per
energy figure is the integral of that dose over the whole scanned
$50\;{\rm mm}$ raster.  This is the $\dot K_{a}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\,
{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}$ peak kerma rate, $\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per
monochromatic $0.02\;{\rm s}$ dwell, $\approx10\!-\!12\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ for
a typical three-energy acquisition, normally quoted for the system.

2.text:
In an air walled chamber the ionisation current is a measure of the
energy which is put into the mass of air which is seen by the beam

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=I\,W/e\qquad W/e\simeq33.97\;{\rm V}
\end{equation*}

and for the mass which is intersected by the beam in the $L=15.1\;{\rm cm}$
chamber

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}
     =1.8\times10^{-2}{\rm cm^{2}} ,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L=1.8\times10^{-2}\times15.1\simeq0.272\;{\rm cm^{3}},
\qquad 
m_{a}= \rho_{a}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}\times0.272
      \simeq3.5\times10^{-4}\ {\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

so that the air kerma rate on the axis of the $0.30\times6.0\;{\rm mm^{2}}$
focus is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \frac{I\,W/e}{m_{a}}
        =\frac{2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97}{3.52\times10^{-7}}
        \simeq1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\dot K_{a}\simeq0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ in air at 33 keV.  With
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}=0.328\;{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$
the same rate is the collision kerma / absorbed dose rate in soft
tissue in electronic equilibrium in the focal spot,

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{a}\simeq1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}} .
\end{equation*}

In a continuous horizontal raster a surface element is in the
$w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ wide spot for

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=
\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/t_{e}} =0.020\;{\rm s}
\end{equation*}

the number quoted in the statement.  The kerma, and to first order the
absorbed dose, actually received by the element during one pass through
the monochromatic focus is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot K_{a}t_{d}\simeq1.93\times10^{-4}\times2.0\times10^{-2}
      \simeq3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per monochromatic 33 keV dwell, i.e. $\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx
0.4\;{\rm mrad}$ air $\simeq$ tissue surface kerma per $20\;{\rm ms}$
dwell in the $0.30\times6.0\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ spot.  In fluence terms

\begin{equation*}
\Phi =\frac{\dot K_{a}}{E(\mu_{en}/\rho)}
     \simeq\frac{1.9\times10^{-4}}
            {33.0\times10^{3}\times1.602\times10^{-19}\times0.0328}
     \simeq1.1\times10^{8}\ {\rm cm^{-2}s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

at 33 keV in the focus, $D_{1}= \Phi E(\mu_{en}/\rho)t_{d}$.

This is the peak surface dose experienced by a material point while it
is actually inside the $0.30\;{\rm mm}$ focus.  For a conventional
two–three energy material decomposition the point dose simply adds,

\begin{equation*}
D_{N}\simeq N D_{1}\simeq 7\!-\!12\;\mu{\rm Gy},
\end{equation*}

e.g. $\simeq3D_{1}\approx1.1\!-\!1.2\times10^{-5}\ {\rm Gy}\approx
10\!-\!12\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ for the usual three energy set, the
$\sim\mu{\rm Gy}$ per projection level normally quoted for synchrotron
micro-imaging.

If the dose is summed over the whole $W_{0}=50\;{\rm mm}$ Laue fan which
is demagnified to the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ focus the number of
independent dwell positions in the horizontal direction is

\begin{equation*}
N_{h}\simeq\frac{W_{0}}{w_{f}}=\frac{50.0}{0.30}\simeq1.67\times10^{2} ,
\end{equation*}

and the total time the moving spot spends building up the
$50\times6\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ radiograph is $T_{scan}=N_{h}t_{d}\simeq3.3\;{\rm
s}$.  The scan integrated, i.e. field–integrated, surface kerma which
is delivered in building up one monochromator fan scan is

\begin{equation*}
D_{field}\simeq N_{h}D_{1}= \dot K_{a}T_{scan}
       \simeq167\times3.9\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
       \simeq6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\;{\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

per mono-energetic 33 keV fan scan, $\simeq0.64\;{\rm mGy}$ with the
numbers quoted above.  The $50/0.30$ geometrical factor cancels for the
per–point quote, so that the conventionally quoted single-projection
surface dose is the $D_{1}\approx3\!-\!4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per $20\;{\rm ms}$
dwell, $\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ peak kerma rate at the focus,
$\approx10\!-\!12\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ for a typical three-energy multiple
energy set.  The $\sim0.6\;{\rm mGy}$ figure is the often quoted
scan–integrated entrance surface dose for the complete $50\;{\rm mm}$
Laue fan. ∎

5.text:
The air kerma which is seen by the chamber in the 0.30$\times$6.0 mm$^{2}$
focus is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}=\frac{I\,W/e}{m_{a}},\qquad 
\frac{W}{e}\simeq 33.97\;{\rm V},
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.80\times10^{-2}
{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L=1.80\times10^{-2}\times15.1=0.272\;{\rm cm^{3}},\qquad
m_{a}= \rho_{a}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}\times0.272\simeq3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}
      =3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg},
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\dot E=I\frac{W}{e}=2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97\simeq6.8\times10^{-11}
{\rm J\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}=\frac{\dot E}{m_{a}}\simeq
\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}\simeq1.9\!-\!2.0\times
10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in air in the focus.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$
this is the peak collision kerma / absorbed dose rate in soft tissue
on the axis of the 33 keV micro beam,

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{a}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\;,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\Phi=\frac{\dot K_{a}}{E(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}}\approx
1.0\!-\!1.1\times10^{8}\ {\rm cm^{-2}s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in the $1.8\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ focal spot.

A material point spends in the stationary $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ wide
focus

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/t_{e}}\simeq
0.020\;{\rm s}
\end{equation*}

in the beam.  The kerma/absorbed dose actually received by a surface
element during one monochromatic dwell is therefore the peak rate
times the geometric dwell,

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot K_{a}t_{d}\simeq1.93\times10^{-4}\times2.0\times10^{-2}
      \simeq3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

in air $\simeq$ tissue, i.e. $\approx 4\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx0.4\;{\rm mrad}$
per 33 keV monochromatic projection.  This is the conventional
synchrotron micro-beam surface kerma, $\dot K_{a}\approx0.19\ {\rm
mGy\,s^{-1}}$ peak, $\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per 20 ms dwell.  For the
usual $N_{E}=2\!-\!3$ energy material decomposition

\begin{equation*}
D_{N_{E}}\approx N_{E}D_{1}\simeq 7\!-\!12\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

$\approx 8\!-\!12\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per point per multi-energy acquisition,
$\approx 10\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ for the conventional three energy set, is the
dose which a given skin element actually experiences while it is
traversed once by the $0.30\;{\rm mm}$ focus.

In terms of the 50 mm$\times$6 mm Laue fan the instantaneous power is
spread over

\begin{equation*}
N_{h}\simeq\frac{W_{m}}{w_{f}}=\frac{50.0}{0.30}\simeq1.67\times10^{2}
\end{equation*}

independent focal positions.  The time to build the whole fan is
$T_{scan}=N_{h}t_{d}\simeq3.3\;{\rm s}$ and the scan-integrated,
field-averaged entrance kerma which is delivered while the moving spot
builds up the $50\times 6\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ radiograph is

\begin{equation*}
D_{\rm field}\simeq N_{h}D_{1}\simeq\dot K_{a}T_{scan}
\approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}{\rm Gy}
\approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\ {\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

per monochromatic fan, $\approx0.64\;{\rm mGy}$ with the numbers quoted
above.  This is the $N_{h}$-folded scan–integrated entrance surface dose
commonly quoted for the Laue fan.  The $4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per 20 ms
monochromatic dwell is the peak per-point surface kerma which is
normally quoted as the synchrotron multiple-energy imaging dose; the
$ \sim 0.6\;{\rm mGy}$ figure is the equivalent scan-integrated entrance
dose for the complete 50 mm horizontal fan.  With $N_{E}\approx2-3$ the
conventional multi-energy point dose is $\approx 8\!-\!12\;\mu{\rm Gy}$,
i.e. of order $10^{-5}\,$Gy, well below conventional diagnostic levels.
∎

6.text:
In an air walled chamber the energy which is put into the air which is
seen by the beam is

\begin{equation*}
\dot E=I\frac{W}{e}\;,\qquad \frac{W}{e}\simeq 33.97\;{\rm V}
\end{equation*}

for air.  With $I=2.0\,{\rm pA}$

\begin{equation*}
\dot E=2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97\simeq 6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm J\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

is the power deposited in the air which is in the $0.30\times6.0\,{\rm
mm^{2}}$ focus.  The air mass which is intersected by the beam in the
$L=15.1\,{\rm cm}$ chamber is

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}\,{\rm cm^{2}},
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L=0.272~{\rm cm^{3}},\qquad
m_{a}=\rho_{a}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}\times0.272\simeq3.5\times10^{-4}\,
{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}\,{\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

so that the air kerma rate on the axis of the focus is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}=\frac{\dot E}{m_{a}}=
\frac{I\,W/e}{m_{a}}\simeq
\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}\simeq
1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in air.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}=0.328
{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$ this is the collision kerma rate which would be
obtained in soft tissue in electronic equilibrium at 33 keV,

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{t}\simeq\dot K_{a}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}},\qquad
\Phi=\frac{\dot K_{a}}{E(\mu_{en}/\rho)}\approx1.1\times10^{8}\ {\rm
} {\rm cm^{-2}s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in the $0.30\times6.0\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ spot.

A material point which is translated through the stationary focus is in
the beam for

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=\frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/t_{e}}=0.020\ {\rm s}
\end{equation*}

in the data.  The kerma actually received by the surface element
during one monochromatic pass through the spot is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}=\dot K_{a}t_{d}\simeq1.93\times10^{-4}\times2.0\times10^{-2}
\simeq3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

in air $\approx$ tissue at 33 keV, i.e. $\approx0.39\,{\rm mrad}$ per
$20\,{\rm ms}$ dwell.  This is the peak surface kerma experienced by a
point while it is inside the $0.30\,{\rm mm}$ micro-beam.  The
conventional two– or three–energy material decomposition is $N_{E}D_{1}$,

\[
D_{N_{E}}\simeq N_{E}\times3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx 7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\;{\rm for\;2\;energies}\approx 11\!-\!12\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\;{\rm for\;3\;energies},
\]

in good agreement with the $\mu$Gy per synchrotron projection normally
quoted for the set-up.  The peak instantaneous surface dose rate at the
focus is $\dot K_{t}\approx0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$.

If the dose is summed over the whole $W_{m}=50\,{\rm mm}$ Laue fan which
is imaged to the $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ focus the number of independent
dwell positions in the horizontal direction is

\[
N_{h}\simeq\frac{W_{m}}{w_{f}}=\frac{50}{0.30}\approx1.67\times10^{2},
\qquad 
T_{scan}=N_{h}t_{d}\approx3.3\;{\rm s},
\]

so that the energy integrated in the moving focal spot during the
complete horizontal raster is

\[
D_{field}= \dot K_{a}T_{scan}=N_{h}D_{1}
      \approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
      \approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\ {\rm mGy}
\]

per monochromatic scan line, $\simeq0.64\,{\rm mGy}$ with the numbers
quoted above, i.e. $\simeq0.6\,{\rm mGy}$ per energy is the
conventionally quoted raster–integrated entrance surface kerma for the
$50\times6\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ field built up by scanning the focus.  The
$A_{m}/A_{f}=W_{m}/w_{f}\approx167$ focusing factor cancels for the mean
surface dose quoted per raster, so that the often quoted per-point
per-energy value remains $D_{1}\approx3\!-\!4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$, $\approx
10\!-\!12\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ for a three-energy multiple-energy acquisition,
the essentially tissue-equivalent air kerma $\dot K_{a}\approx
0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ being the basic dosimetric quantity at the
33 keV focus.  This is the $\sim10^{-6}$ Gy per projection order of
magnitude normally quoted for synchrotron micro-imaging. ∎

8.text:
In air the ionisation current is a measure of the power which is put into
the mass of air which is seen by the beam

\begin{equation*}
{\dot E}=I\,W/e\qquad W/e\simeq33.97\;{\rm V}
\end{equation*}

and for the mass of air which is in the $0.30\times 6.0\;{\rm mm^{2}}$
focus in the $L=15.1\;{\rm cm}$ chamber

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.80\times10^{-2}\;{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L=0.272\;{\rm cm^{3}},\qquad 
m_{a}=\rho_{a}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}\times0.272\simeq3.5\times10^{-4}\;
{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}\;{\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

the air kerma rate on the axis of the focus is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \frac{{\dot E}}{m_{a}}=\frac{I\,W/e}{m_{a}}
      =\frac{2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
      \simeq1.9-2.0\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in air.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}=0.328\,
{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$ at 33 keV the collision kerma rate and hence the
absorbed dose rate in soft tissue in electronic equilibrium at the
focus is the same to first order,

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{a}\simeq1.9\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in the $0.30\times6.0\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ spot.  In terms of photon fluence

\begin{equation*}
\Phi =\frac{\dot K_{a}}{E(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}}\approx
\frac{1.9\times10^{-4}}{5.29\times10^{-15}\times0.0328}
\approx1.1\times10^{8}\;{\rm cm^{-2}s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

at 33 keV, $ \dot K_{a}\approx\Phi E(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$, in the focus.

A material point which is translated through the stationary $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm
mm}$ spot stays in the beam for

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=\frac{w_{f}}{{\rm step}/t_{e}}
      \approx0.020\;{\rm s}
\end{equation*}

the time quoted for the width/scan speed ratio.  The kerma actually
received by a surface element on one pass through the beam is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot K_{a}t_{d}\simeq1.93\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times
2.0\times10^{-2}\;{\rm s}\simeq3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\;{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\simeq4\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx0.4\;{\rm mrad}$ air $\simeq$ tissue
surface kerma per monochromatic $33\;{\rm keV}$ projection in the
$0.30\times6.0\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ microbeam.  This is the $\sim\mu{\rm Gy}$
per projection surface dose normally quoted for synchrotron micro-imaging.
For a conventional $N_{E}=2\!-\!3$ energy material decomposition the
point dose is $N_{E}D_{1}\sim7\!-\!12\;\mu{\rm Gy}$, $\sim1.2\times10^{-5}\;{\rm
Gy}$ for three energies, essentially the same $ \dot K_{a}\approx0.19\;{\rm
mGy\,s^{-1}}$ peak rate at the focus.

In the Laue geometry the $W_{0}=50\;{\rm mm}$ horizontal acceptance is
imaged to the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ focus,

\begin{equation*}
N_{h}=\frac{W_{0}}{w_{f}}\approx\frac{50}{0.30}\approx1.67\times10^{2}
\end{equation*}

independent dwell positions per scan line.  The time the moving spot
spends building up the $50\;{\rm mm}$ fan is $T_{scan}=N_{h}t_{d}\approx
3.3\;{\rm s}$ and the time-integrated, field-averaged, entrance kerma
which is delivered in building up the whole $50\times6\;{\rm mm^{2}}$
radiograph is

\begin{equation*}
D_{{\rm field}}\simeq N_{h}D_{1}= \dot K_{a}T_{scan}
      \approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}\;{\rm Gy}
      \approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\;{\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

per monochromatic scan line, $\approx0.64\;{\rm mGy}$ with the numbers
above, i.e. $\approx0.6\;{\rm mGy}$ per energy for the full $50\;{\rm mm}$
fan.  The $D_{1}\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per $20\;{\rm ms}$ dwell is the
peak surface kerma experienced by an individual skin element; the
$N_{h}D_{1}\sim0.6\;{\rm mGy}$ is the conventionally quoted
scan-integrated entrance surface kerma for the $50\;{\rm mm}$ Laue fan.
With three energies the field-integrated entrance dose is $\sim1.8\!-\!2.0\;
{\rm mGy}$.  This is the $\sim\mu{\rm Gy}$ per projection, $\sim{\rm mGy}$
field-integrated, dose level usually quoted for synchrotron multiple-
energy micro-imaging. ∎

public_text.3:
In an air walled chamber

* $W/e\simeq33.97\;{\rm V}$ for air

$\dot E=I\,W/e$ is the power put into the air which is seen by the beam

\begin{equation*}
\dot E=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97{\rm V}
      \simeq6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm J\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in the beam. At the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}
     =1.8\times10^{-2}\ {\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

so that with the $L=15.1\,{\rm cm}$ active length

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L\simeq0.272\ {\rm cm^{3}},\qquad 
m_{a}= \rho_{a}V_{f}
     =1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times0.272{\rm cm^{3}}
     \simeq3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

is the mass of air which is irradiated.  The air kerma rate on the
axis of the $0.30\times6.0\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ focus is therefore

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \frac{I\,W/e}{m_{a}}
        \simeq\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
        \simeq1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
        \approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in air.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}=0.328\,
{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$ at 33 keV

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}\simeq\dot D_{t}\simeq\Phi E(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}
\end{equation*}

is the collision kerma / absorbed dose rate in soft tissue in the
focus, $\Phi\approx1.1\times10^{8}\ {\rm cm^{-2}s^{-1}}$ in the
$0.3\times6\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ spot.

For a point of tissue which is translated through the stationary
focus the illumination time is

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/t_{e}}
\end{equation*}

which is quoted as

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=w_{f}/\bigl(\Delta x/t_{e}\bigr)=0.020\ {\rm s}
\end{equation*}

for the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ spot.  The kerma actually received by
that surface element in one monochromatic pass is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot K_{a}\,t_{d}
     \simeq1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}
     \simeq3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\approx3\!-\!4\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx0.4\;{\rm mrad}$ per $33\,{\rm keV}$
projection at the $2.0\;{\rm pA}$ operating point, essentially the
air$\simeq$tissue entrance surface dose.  This is the peak dose a
given skin element experiences while it is actually inside the
$0.30\,{\rm mm}$ focal spot for the quoted $20\,{\rm ms}$ dwell.

In particular

* $\dot K_{a}\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ on axis,
* $D_{1}\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per monochromatic dwell.

For the conventional 2–3 energy multiple-energy acquisition the point
dose simply adds, $D_{N}\simeq N D_{1}$, i.e. $\simeq7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$
for a two-energy set and $\simeq1.0\!-\!1.2\times10^{-5}\ {\rm Gy}\approx
10\!-\!12\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ for the usual three-energy material
decomposition, the $\mu{\rm Gy}$ per projection level normally quoted
for synchrotron micro-imaging.

Remark on the field-integrated number.  The $50\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm
mm}$ monochromator fan is imaged to the $0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm
mm}$ focus, $N_{h}=W_{m}/w_{f}=50/0.30\simeq1.67\times10^{2}$ independent
dwell positions.  The total time needed to raster the whole fan is
$T_{\rm scan}=N_{h}t_{d}\simeq3.3\,{\rm s}$ and the energy deposited in
the moving focal spot while the $50\,{\rm mm}$ field is built up is

\begin{equation*}
D_{\rm field}\simeq N_{h}D_{1}\simeq\dot K_{a}T_{\rm scan}
        \approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
        \approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\ {\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

per monochromatic fan scan, $\approx0.64\;{\rm mGy}$ with the numbers
above.  This $\sim0.6\;{\rm mGy}$ is the conventionally quoted
scan-integrated entrance surface dose for the $50\,{\rm mm}$ field; the
$ \approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy, $\sim10\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ for a
2–3-energy set, is the physically relevant dose actually experienced
by a single tissue element as it passes once through the $0.30\,{\rm mm}$
focus. ∎

public_text.1:
In the air walled chamber the charge which is collected is the charge
produced by the energy put into the air which is in the beam

\begin{equation*}
\dot E =I\,\frac{W}{e}, \qquad \frac{W}{e}\simeq 33.97\;{\rm V},
\end{equation*}

so that

\begin{equation*}
\dot E =2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97\,{\rm V}
      \simeq6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm J\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

is the power absorbed in the air intersected by the beam.  In the
focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}\ {\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

and with the $L=15.1\;{\rm cm}$ active length

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L\simeq0.272\ {\rm cm^{3}},\qquad
m_{a}= \rho_{a}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}\times0.272
     \simeq3.5\times10^{-4}\ {\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

so that the air kerma rate on the axis of the $0.30\times6.0\;{\rm
mm^{2}}$ focal spot is the usual $I\,W/e$ air kerma

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \frac{I\,W/e}{m_{a}}
        \simeq\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
        \simeq1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\approx0.19\ {\rm
mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in air.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}=0.328\,
{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$ at $33\;{\rm keV}$ this is the collision kerma, and
essentially the absorbed dose rate in soft tissue in electronic
equilibrium in the focal spot.

The dwell of a material point in the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ wide spot
in a continuous horizontal raster is

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/t_{e}},
\end{equation*}

the quantity quoted in the statement, $t_{d}=0.020\;{\rm s}$.  The
kerma, and with $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ the
absorbed dose, actually received by a surface element during one pass
through the beam and for one monochromatic energy is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot K_{a}\,t_{d}
     \simeq1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}
     \simeq3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per $33\;{\rm keV}$ exposure.  In fluence terms

\begin{equation*}
\Phi =\frac{\dot K_{a}}{E(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}}
     \approx\frac{1.9\times10^{-4}}
                {5.29\times10^{-15}\times0.0328}
     \approx1.1\times10^{8}\ {\rm cm^{-2}s^{-1}},
\end{equation*}

$D_{1}=\Phi E(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}t_{d}$, i.e. $\approx0.39\;{\rm mrad}$
per projection.  For a conventional $N_{E}$-energy material
decomposition the point dose simply adds,

\begin{equation*}
D_{N}\simeq N_{E}D_{1},
\end{equation*}

e.g. $D_{2}\simeq7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ for two energies and
$D_{3}\simeq1.1\!-\!1.2\times10^{-5}\ {\rm Gy}\approx10\!-\!12\;\mu{\rm
Gy}$ for the usual three energy set, the $\sim\mu{\rm Gy}$ per
projection dose normally quoted for synchrotron micro-imaging.

The $50\;{\rm mm}\times6\;{\rm mm}$ monochromator acceptance is
geometrically compressed by $M=w_{f}/W_{m}=0.30/50.0=6.0\times10^{-3}$ to
the $0.30\times6.0\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ focus.  The number of independent
focal positions required to build up the $W_{m}=50\;{\rm mm}$ field is

\begin{equation*}
N_{h}\simeq\frac{W_{m}}{w_{f}}=\frac{50}{0.30}\approx1.67\times10^{2},
\end{equation*}

so that the time needed to scan the whole fan is
$T_{scan}=N_{h}t_{d}\approx3.33\;{\rm s}$.  The energy which is
delivered to the moving focal spot while the $50\;{\rm mm}$ fan is
built up is

\begin{equation*}
D_{field}\simeq \dot K_{a}T_{scan}=N_{h}D_{1}
          \approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
          \approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\ {\rm
mGy}
\end{equation*}

per monochromatic energy, $\approx0.64\;{\rm mGy}$ with the numbers
above, i.e. the conventionally quoted field-integrated entrance surface
dose for the $50\;{\rm mm}\times6\;{\rm mm}$ Laue fan.  The $D_{1}
\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per $20\;{\rm ms}$ dwell is the dose actually
experienced by any single skin element as it traverses the
$0.30\times6.0\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ microbeam; the $ \sim0.6\;{\rm mGy}$ per
energy figure is the integral of that dose over the whole scanned
$50\;{\rm mm}$ raster.  This is the $\dot K_{a}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\,
{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}$ peak kerma rate, $\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per
monochromatic $0.02\;{\rm s}$ dwell, $\approx10\!-\!12\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ for
a typical three-energy acquisition, normally quoted for the system.

