air$\simeq$tissue per 33 keV dwell, i.e. $\simeq0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}\times
0.02\,{\rm s}$.  That is the dose which a given skin element actually
experiences while it is inside the $0.30\times6.0\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ micro
beam.  For a conventional $N_{E}$-energy material decomposition the
point dose adds,

\begin{equation*}
D_{N_{E}}\simeq N_{E}D_{1}\approx N_{E}\times4\;\mu{\rm Gy},
\end{equation*}

e.g. $D_{2}\simeq7\!-\!8\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ for two energies and
$D_{3}\simeq1.1\!-\!1.2\times10^{-5}\,{\rm Gy}\approx10\!-\!12\,\mu{\rm Gy}$
for the usual three-energy set, the $\sim\mu{\rm Gy}$ per projection
synchrotron micro-imaging level normally quoted for the system.

The geometrical demagnification is $W_{m}/w_{f}=50/0.30\simeq1.67\times10^{2}$,
$N_{h}\simeq W_{m}/w_{f}\approx167$ independent focal positions are
required to raster the $50\times6\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ Laue fan.  The time
needed to build up the whole fan is $T_{scan}=N_{h}t_{d}\simeq3.33\,{\rm
s}$ and the energy which is deposited in the moving focal spot while the
$50\,{\rm mm}$ fan is built up is

\begin{equation*}
D_{field}=N_{h}D_{1}=\dot K_{a}T_{scan}\simeq167\times3.9\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\simeq6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\ {\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

per monochromatic 33 keV scan line, $\approx0.64\,{\rm mGy}$ with the
numbers above.  That is the scan-integrated, field-built entrance
kerma for the $50\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm mm}$ raster, $ \sim1\!-\!2\,{\rm mGy}$
for a 2-3 energy set, $ \sim1.9\,{\rm mGy}$ for three energies.  The
$D_{1}\approx4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per $20\,{\rm ms}$ dwell per energy is the
physically relevant peak surface kerma experienced by a single tissue
element as it traverses the focus once, the $N_{h}D_{1}\approx0.6\,{\rm
mGy}$ per energy being the conventionally quoted scan-integrated
entrance surface dose for the complete $50\,{\rm mm}$ Laue fan. ∎
